.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt PGREP 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm pgrep ,
.Nm pkill
.Nd find or signal processes by name
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm pgrep
.Op Fl aLSfilnoqvx
.Op Fl d Ar delim
.Op Fl F Ar pidfile
.Op Fl G Ar gid
.Op Fl P Ar ppid
.Op Fl U Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar pgrp
.Op Fl s Ar sid
.Op Fl t Ar tty
.Op Fl u Ar euid
.Ar pattern ...
.Nm pkill
.Op Fl Ar signal
.Op Fl aILSfilnovx
.Op Fl F Ar pidfile
.Op Fl G Ar gid
.Op Fl P Ar ppid
.Op Fl U Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar pgrp
.Op Fl s Ar sid
.Op Fl t Ar tty
.Op Fl u Ar euid
.Ar pattern ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm pgrep
looks through the running processes and writes the process ID of each that
matches, one per line.
.Nm pkill
sends a signal to each instead.
.Pp
A process matches when its name matches any
.Ar pattern ,
an extended regular expression
.Pq Xr re_format 7 ,
and it is in every list given by the options below.
The patterns may be left out when an option selects.
The program itself is never picked.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Ar signal
.Pq Nm pkill No only, and first
The signal to send, by name (with or without
.Ql SIG ,
in any case) or number;
.Dv SIGTERM
by default.
.It Fl a
Include the program's own ancestors, which are left out otherwise.
.It Fl d Ar delim
.Pq Nm pgrep
Separate the process IDs with
.Ar delim
instead of a newline.
.It Fl F Ar pidfile
Only the process whose ID is in
.Ar pidfile .
.It Fl f
Match against the whole argument list, not just the name.
.It Fl G Ar gid
Processes whose real group is one of those listed.
.It Fl g Ar pgrp
Processes in one of the process groups listed; 0 is the program's own.
.It Fl I
.Pq Nm pkill
Ask before signalling each process.
.It Fl i
Ignore case in the patterns.
.It Fl L
With
.Fl F ,
fail unless
.Ar pidfile
is locked, as a running daemon keeps its pid file
.Pq Xr flock 2 .
.It Fl l
Long output:
.Nm pgrep
writes the name after each process ID, or with
.Fl f
the arguments;
.Nm pkill
writes the kill it did for each.
.It Fl n
Only the newest of the processes picked.
.It Fl o
Only the oldest of the processes picked.
.It Fl P Ar ppid
Processes whose parent is one of those listed.
.It Fl q
.Pq Nm pgrep
Write nothing; just exit with the status.
.It Fl S
Include system processes (kernel threads, where the system has them).
.It Fl s Ar sid
Processes in one of the sessions listed; 0 is the program's own.
.It Fl t Ar tty
Processes whose controlling terminal is one of those listed, named as
.Xr ps 1 Ns 's
.Fl t
takes them;
.Ql -
for none.
.It Fl U Ar uid
Processes whose real user is one of those listed, by name or number.
.It Fl u Ar euid
Processes whose effective user is one of those listed.
.It Fl v
Pick the processes that do not match instead.
.It Fl x
The pattern must match all of the name (or with
.Fl f ,
the arguments).
.El
.Pp
A list is separated by commas.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
One or more processes were picked (and, for
.Nm pkill ,
signalled).
.It 1
None were.
.It 2
The command line was wrong.
.It 3
Something else failed.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The process IDs of every
.Xr sh 1 :
.Dl $ pgrep -x sh
.Pp
Ask the system logger to read its configuration again:
.Dl # pkill -HUP -F /var/run/syslog.pid
.Pp
Whether any of a user's processes run a given script:
.Dl $ pgrep -q -U operator -f 'backup\e.sh' && echo running
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr re_format 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm pkill
and
.Nm pgrep
first appeared in Solaris 7, then in
.Nx 1.6
and
.Fx 5.3 .
These are written in Rust for OxideBSD.
